{"schema":2,"commit":"e9bfa1217bcc09f8196f1b17ac2609c46293f2e2","branch":"master","base_sha":"1a37e52eda6cfe32f2c8e3a505ba22e014d49485","message":"Meter DC charging only, on a monotonic clock\n\nThe charging session meter used the head unit's wall clock, which jumps when it\npicks up GPS or NTP time. One forward jump mid-charge invented both kWh and\nhours. The consumption counters already integrate on elapsedRealtime for exactly\nthis reason; the session meter now does too, and keeps wall-clock values only as\nthe start and end stamps a reader needs to place a session on a timeline.\n\nIntervals longer than five minutes are no longer bridged. A head unit that slept\nmid-charge came back and billed the whole gap at the last power reading, and a\nsession interrupted overnight reported the night as charging time. Reported\nduration is metered charging time now, not the span from start to end.\n\nSessions are metered on DC alone, at or above 10 kW. AC charging on this car is\nan overnight affair the head unit sleeps through, so its curve, duration and\nenergy were guesses either way. The charging screen says so rather than letting\nthe previous DC session's figures be read as the current AC charge.\n\nAlso:\n\n- A daily consumption record uploaded after an offline spell carries the\n  lifetime total as it stood at the close of that day. Every backlogged day used\n  to be stamped with the current lifetime, which plots as a flat line and one\n  cliff.\n- The offline session queue is capped at 50. It is a single SharedPreferences\n  string read into memory in full, and nothing drained it while the cloud\n  endpoint was unconfigured.\n- A long charge keeps the start of its curve: at 720 points the sample interval\n  doubles and the curve is thinned, rather than dropping the oldest point.\n- The cloud sync throttle runs on the monotonic clock, and catch-up sends up to\n  10 days and 10 sessions per cycle rather than one and three.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M contex